# The design containers: ~60MB, and the real source of truth for every page.
# Gitignored to match the Stellar project's convention — but that also means a
# revert restores dist/ and NOT these, so a rebuild can silently re-ship a
# reverted change. Back them up before any transform that deletes rather than adds.
lumoscore-xrpl-*.html
node_modules/

# Secrets. The xrpl.to API key is a SERVER-side credential — unlike the Xaman API key, which is a
# public PKCE client id and ships in the page on purpose. This one is read from the environment:
# XRPLTO_API_KEY, set in Cloudflare Pages for production and exported in the shell for local runs.
# It must never be written into a file in this repo.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
_containers_backup/

# wrangler build cache — holds the Cloudflare account id/name, not source
.wrangler/

# container snapshots (huge, and regenerable by re-running the transforms)
_containers_broken_20260908/
# BUILD OUTPUT, NONE OF IT TRACKED, AND dist/ JOINED THE LIST ON 2026-10-13.
#
# dist/xrpl/ and .deploy/ were already here, which left the repo tracking dist/ alone -- the ROOT
# build, produced by `npm run build` (extract_site.js xrpl --root). That is the build staging does NOT
# use: combine_with_stellar.js copies from dist/xrpl/, so the tracked copy was never the deployed one
# and nothing kept it current. Measured the day it was removed, on the same bridge page:
#
#   dist/lumoscore-bridge.html       04:34   no Hedera fix, delivery badge still in
#   dist/xrpl/lumoscore-bridge.html  23:08   both correct, and the one actually serving
#
# A stale copy of the wrong build is worse than no copy. It reads as the artifact, it survives a
# revert while the containers do not (see the note at the top of this file), and the one thing it is
# good for -- deploying -- would ship yesterday's pages from a directory nobody rebuilt. I had already
# committed it once describing it as carrying a fix it did not contain.
#
# Nothing deploys from git here: lumoscore-xrpl.git is connected to no Pages project, and staging goes
# up as a direct wrangler upload from disk. So untracking costs nothing and removes a trap.
#
# Rebuild it with the documented chain, never from a checkout:
#   node _tools/rebuild.js && node _tools/extract_site.js xrpl && node _tools/combine_with_stellar.js --write
dist/
dist-combined/
functions-combined/
.deploy/
